# AI and Machine Learning Basics: How Machines Learn From Data

> Traditional programming feeds rules and data in to get answers;
> machine learning feeds data and answers in to get rules. That
> single inversion now powers search, translation, recommendations
> and chat assistants — and rests on surprisingly few ideas: a model,
> a loss, and a loop that keeps making the loss smaller.

## 1. Rule-Based AI versus Machine Learning

[definition]
**Artificial intelligence** is the broad goal of machines performing
tasks that need intelligence. **Machine learning (ML)** achieves that
goal differently from classic programming: instead of hand-coding
rules, the system **fits a model to data** — parameters are adjusted
until predictions match known examples.
[/definition]

| | Classic programming | Machine learning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Input | rules + data | data + labels |
| Output | answers | rules (a trained model) |
| Good when | logic is known, stable | patterns are complex, shifting |
| Weakness | can't enumerate every case | needs data; can inherit its biases |

## 2. The Three Learning Paradigms

- **Supervised learning** — the data carries labels (answers). Two
  flavours: **regression** predicts a continuous number (house price)
  and **classification** picks a category (spam / not spam).
- **Unsupervised learning** — no labels; the model finds structure:
  **clustering** (group similar customers), dimensionality reduction
  (compress features), association rules.
- **Reinforcement learning (RL)** — an **agent** acts in an
  **environment**, receives **rewards**, and learns a **policy** that
  maximises long-run reward (game playing, robotics, resource
  management).

[example]
Predicting tomorrow's temperature from history: regression. Flagging a
transaction fraudulent or genuine: classification. Segmenting
shoppers with no labels at all: clustering. Teaching a robot to walk
from reward signals only: reinforcement learning. Most exam questions
reduce to exactly this four-way mapping.
[/example]

## 3. The Anatomy of Learning

1. Collect and clean data; split into **training**, **validation**
   and **test** sets (a typical 70/15/15).
2. Choose a **model** — a family of adjustable functions (a line, a
   tree, a neural network).
3. Define a **loss function** — a number measuring how wrong the
   model currently is (mean squared error for regression,
   cross-entropy for classification).
4. **Train**: adjust parameters to shrink the loss on the training
   set, typically by **gradient descent** — repeatedly nudging every
   parameter a small step downhill against the loss's slope.
5. **Validate/tune** hyperparameters on the validation set; finally
   **evaluate** once on the untouched test set.

@math label="eq:gradient-descent"
w_{\text{new}} = w_{\text{old}} - \eta \, \frac{\partial L}{\partial w}
@/math

where $\eta$ is the **learning rate**: too large and training
oscillates or diverges; too small and it crawls.

## 4. Overfitting: The Central Disease

[important]
**Overfitting** is memorising the training set instead of learning
the pattern: training loss keeps falling while validation loss rises.
Its mirror, **underfitting**, happens when the model is too simple to
capture the signal even in training. The fix for overfitting is more
data, a simpler model, regularisation (penalise large weights), or
early stopping; the fix for underfitting is more capacity or better
features.
[/important]

**Bias–variance trade-off:** high-bias models are too rigid
(underfit), high-variance models chase noise (overfit). The goal is
the sweet spot — and honest measurement demands the test set be
touched exactly once, at the end.

## 5. Common Algorithms and When They Fit

| Algorithm | Type | Strength |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Linear/logistic regression | supervised | interpretable baseline |
| Decision trees | supervised | human-readable rules |
| Random forests / gradient boosting | supervised | strong tabular performers |
| k-means | unsupervised | simple clustering |
| k-NN | supervised | no training phase; lazy learner |
| Neural networks | supervised/RL | images, speech, language |

**Feature engineering** — scaling, encoding categories, crafting
informative inputs — often matters more than the algorithm choice on
tabular data; a raw model on good features beats a fancy model on
raw columns.

## 6. Neural Networks and Deep Learning

A neuron computes a weighted sum plus bias, then applies a
non-linear **activation** (ReLU, sigmoid). Stacking neurons in layers
creates a network; many layers make it **deep**. Training uses
**backpropagation**: run the gradient of the loss backward through
every layer to know which way each weight should move.

Depth buys automatic feature hierarchy: early layers learn edges,
later layers learn eyes, faces and objects. Deep learning dominates
vision, speech and language, but it is data-hungry, compute-hungry
and opaque — hence the popularity of **transfer learning** (start
from a pre-trained model and fine-tune on a small dataset) and the
rise of **transformers** for language tasks.

## 7. Measuring Quality: Beyond Accuracy

On imbalanced data (1% fraud), predicting "never fraud" scores 99%
accuracy and is useless. Better instruments:

- **Confusion matrix** — TP, FP, TN, FN.
- **Precision** $= \frac{TP}{TP + FP}$: of the alarms raised, how
  many were real?
- **Recall** $= \frac{TP}{TP + FN}$: of the real cases, how many were
  caught?
- **F1** — their harmonic mean; **ROC-AUC** — ranking quality across
  thresholds.

**Worked example.** A classifier catching 80 of 100 frauds (recall
0.80) while misflagging 200 honest transactions among 10,000:
precision $= \frac{80}{280} \approx 0.29$. Whether that is acceptable
depends on the cost of a missed fraud versus a blocked customer — a
business decision, not a mathematical one.

## 8. Ethics, Limits and the Road Ahead

Models inherit the biases of their training data (hiring models that
absorb historical discrimination); opaque models fail high-stakes
accountability (hence XAI — explainable AI); generative models can
hallucinate confident nonsense. Meanwhile deployment concerns — data
drift, monitoring, feedback loops — dominate real-world failure
modes. The discipline's future is less about bigger models than
about safer, more frugal, better-governed ones.

## 9. Summary

Machine learning replaces hand-written rules with parameters fitted
to data. Supervised learning maps inputs to known labels,
unsupervised learning finds structure, reinforcement learning learns
from reward. Training is nothing more than minimising a loss by
gradient descent, and the craft lies in the boring parts: clean data,
honest splits, the right bias–variance balance and metrics that
reflect real costs.
